     1	# Classification of Fermat-spiral limit types
     2	
     3	Date: 2026-10-14 PT. **Verdict: SOLVED for the exact type/covolume questions in Theorems 1–2. Evidence label: Certification by a written proof.** The local-lattice mechanism is classical (Bacher 2014); no new approximation method or worldwide first-priority claim is made. An independent referee found no mathematical gap; attribution/source clarifications and finite controls are recorded below. This is not a Lean/kernel certificate.
     4	
     5	## 1. Exact statements
     6	
     7	Identify the plane with C. For a fixed real alpha put
     8	
     9	`P_n=sqrt(n)*exp(2*pi*i*n*alpha)`, `X_alpha={P_n:n>=1}`.
    10	
    11	A translation limit means `X_alpha+t_j -> Y` in the Chabauty–Fell topology with `|t_j| -> infinity`. There are **no rescalings or rotations in this definition**. We use local closed-set convergence: for every fixed R and epsilon, eventually each point of either set in the radius-R ball lies within epsilon of the other **full** set. This formulation avoids artificial boundary errors from intersecting both sets with the same ball.
    12	
    13	This is equivalent to Tevet's metric formulation: for a requested metric error epsilon use the fixed radius `1/epsilon` (or a slightly larger radius) and a smaller local error; conversely a metric error smaller than both epsilon and `1/R` supplies the fixed-radius inclusions. We also use continuity of translations by convergent vectors and of invertible linear maps converging to an invertible map. On a fixed ball, the preimages remain in a larger bounded ball and the displacement errors are bounded by the vector error plus the matrix-norm error times that larger radius.
    14	
    15	**Theorem 1 (types and covolume).** For every real alpha, every nonempty translation limit of X_alpha is either an affine line or an affine rank-two lattice with covolume **pi**. Thus no translate of a point, an infinite cyclic discrete group, `R x Z`, or `R^2` occurs.
    16	
    17	**Theorem 2 (realization of types for the question's class).** For every fixed irrational alpha that is not badly approximable:
    18	
    19	1. a lattice limit of covolume pi exists;
    20	2. **every affine line** is a translation limit;
    21	3. the empty set is a translation limit.
    22	
    23	Consequently, the exact possible **types** are empty, affine line, and affine rank-two lattice of covolume pi, and all three types occur for each such alpha. This does **not** assert that every covolume-pi lattice shape occurs for a fixed alpha. That is a different realization question.
    24	
    25	The theorem proves Tevet's p. 4 containment **in sharpened form** and gives a complete proof of the affirmative p. 12 covolume answer. Read together with his one-to-one local description, Bacher's Remark 1.2(2) already asserts that answer in Voronoi-area form: under the nonmerging convergence at a lattice limit, the local Voronoi cells converge to its fundamental cell. That is an implication of his statements, not a separately named Chabauty theorem; the remark's area assertion is unproved. Our proof instead uses the explicit fixed-area lattices. It **excludes `Z x R`**, contrary to the p. 12 computational suggestion that all proposed types are achieved. We distinguish the containment reading from the suggestion of an exact attainable list.
    26	
    27	**Boundary statements.** Every irrational alpha, including BA alpha, has at least one lattice limit. BA alpha has only lattices as nonempty limits. Rational alpha has only affine-line nonempty limits, parallel to one of its finitely many radial directions; it also has empty limits. Adding the point P_0=0 to the source changes none of these limits.
    28	
    29	## 2. Uniform, two-sided local lattice approximation
    30	
    31	For `r=sqrt(n)` define the frame-rotated centered set and a genuine lattice
    32	
    33	```text
    34	Y_n = exp(-2*pi*i*n*alpha)*(X_alpha-P_n),
    35	L_n = { v_n(q,p) : (q,p) in Z^2 },
    36	v_n(q,p) = (q/(2*r), 2*pi*r*(q*alpha-p)).
    37	```
    38	
    39	The basis matrix is
    40	
    41	```text
    42	[[ 1/(2*r),       0    ],
    43	 [ 2*pi*r*alpha, -2*pi*r ]],
    44	```
    45	
    46	whose determinant is `-pi`. Therefore every L_n has covolume **exactly pi**, with no Diophantine hypothesis.
    47	
    48	**Lemma 2.1 (forward inclusion).** If R>0, `r>=4R` and `|v_n(q,p)|<=R`, then `n+q>=1`, p is the unique nearest integer to q*alpha, and
    49	
    50	`| sqrt(n+q)*exp(2*pi*i*(q*alpha-p))-r-v_n(q,p) | <= 5R^2/r`.
    51	
    52	Thus `L_n intersect B_R` lies in the `5R^2/r` neighborhood of Y_n.
    53	
    54	**Proof.** Write `a=q/(2r)`, `b=2*pi*r*(q*alpha-p)`, `theta=b/r`, `s=sqrt(r^2+q)`, `u=s-r`. Since `|q|<=2rR<=r^2/2`, the integer `n+q` is strictly positive and hence at least one. Also `|q*alpha-p|<=1/(8*pi)<1/2`, which proves the nearest-integer assertion.
    55	
    56	The exact radial identity is `a=u+u^2/(2r)`, and `|u|=|q|/(s+r)<=2|a|`. Using the elementary integral-remainder inequalities
    57	
    58	`|exp(i theta)-1|<=|theta|`,
    59	
    60	`|exp(i theta)-1-i theta|<=theta^2/2`,
    61	
    62	we obtain
    63	
    64	```text
    65	s*exp(i theta)-r-(a+i*b)
    66	 = (u-a)+u*(exp(i theta)-1)+r*(exp(i theta)-1-i theta),
    67	
    68	absolute value <= (2*a^2+2*|a*b|+b^2/2)/r
    69	               <= (3*a^2+3*b^2/2)/r
    70	               <= 3*R^2/r <= 5*R^2/r.
    71	```
    72	
    73	The advertised constant five is deliberately conservative. All estimates are uniform in alpha. QED.
    74	
    75	**Lemma 2.2 (reverse inclusion).** Suppose `m=n+q>=1`, `|P_m-P_n|<=R`, and `r>=12R`. Let p be the nearest integer to q*alpha. Then
    76	
    77	`|v_n(q,p)|<3R`
    78	
    79	and the same physical/frame displacement differs from v_n(q,p) by at most `45R^2/r`. Thus `Y_n intersect B_R` lies in the `45R^2/r` neighborhood of L_n.
    80	
    81	**Proof.** Set `z=exp(-2*pi*i*n*alpha)*(P_m-P_n)` and `s=sqrt(m)`. The reverse triangle inequality gives `|s-r|<=R`, hence
    82	
    83	`|q/(2r)|=|s-r|*(s+r)/(2r)<=R*(1+R/(2r))<=25R/24`.
    84	
    85	Since `Re(r+z)>=r-R>0`, the principal argument theta of r+z lies strictly between `-pi/2` and `pi/2`. Moreover
    86	
    87	`|theta|<=|Im z|/(r-R)<=R/(r-R)`.
    88	
    89	It is the unique representative of `2*pi*q*alpha` in this interval, so it selects the unique nearest integer p, and
    90	
    91	`|2*pi*r*(q*alpha-p)|=r*|theta|<=12R/11`.
    92	
    93	Therefore `|v|<=|a|+|b|<3R`. Apply Lemma 2.1 at radius 3R: its requirement is exactly `r>=12R`, and its error is `5*(3R)^2/r=45R^2/r`. QED.
    94	
    95	**Corollary 2.3.** For any sequence `n_j -> infinity`, the centered physical sets `X_alpha-P_(n_j)` and the rotated lattices
    96	
    97	`Lambda_j=exp(2*pi*i*n_j*alpha)*L_(n_j)`
