    11	### 1.1 Fermionic parameters versus the superspace coordinate
    12	
    13	Let K be R or C and let `A=Lambda_K(zeta_1,...,zeta_r)` be a finite exterior algebra,
    14	with its usual Z/2 grading; r may be any finite integer. The generators anticommute
    15	and square to zero. The **external** odd coordinate theta is a further independent
    16	generator. Coefficients are smooth scalar functions of (x,y,t), extended by A and theta.
    17	All tau functions F,G are even in total parity. Write theta to the left when expanding:
    18	`F=F_even+theta F_odd`, with `F_even in C^infinity(x,y,t;K) tensor A_even` and
    19	`F_odd in C^infinity(x,y,t;K) tensor A_odd`. Scalar-function tensor factors are
    20	understood in the algebra maps below.
    21	
    22	Define the parameter augmentation
    23	
    24	    pi_zeta: A tensor Lambda(theta) -> Lambda(theta),
    25	    zeta_i -> 0,     theta -> theta.
    26	
    27	This is NOT the full projection setting theta to zero. Since F,G are even, their
    28	images `f=pi_zeta(F)`, `g=pi_zeta(G)` are scalar and theta-independent: an odd scalar
    29	coefficient of theta cannot survive augmentation.
    30	
    31	Use **left** Grassmann derivatives: for homogeneous u,
    32	`partial_theta(uv)=(partial_theta u)v+(-1)^parity(u)u(partial_theta v)`.
    33	The superderivative is `D_theta=partial_theta+theta partial_y`, exactly as in
    34	Wang–Tang–Liang2018 §2.1, p. 040203-2. With independent external theta_1,theta_2,
    35	shared parameter algebra A, and diagonal evaluation Delta, define
    36	
    37	    P_n = D_t + D_x^n,
    38	    P_n F·G = Delta[(partial_t1-partial_t2
