   107	## 2. Parameter-body projection theorem
   108	
   109	**Theorem 2.1 (projection retaining theta).** For any constant-coefficient polynomial
   110	P in the ordinary Hirota operators D_x,D_t and any even F,G as above,
   111	
   112	    pi_zeta(P F·G)=P f·g,
   113	    pi_zeta(S_y P F·G)=theta D_y P f·g.                 (2.1)
   114	
   115	Consequently, any solution of the paired supersymmetric equations projects to a
   116	solution `P f·g=0`, `D_y P f·g=0` of the bosonic pair.
   117	
   118	**Proof.** The map `pi_zeta=augmentation tensor identity` is an even superalgebra
   119	homomorphism. The ideal I generated by all parameter generators zeta_i is preserved
   120	by ordinary derivatives and by `partial_theta1,partial_theta2`: writing external
   121	theta monomials to the left, these derivatives act only on the external-coordinate
   122	factor and never remove a parameter generator. Multiplication by theta_i preserves
   123	I as well. The diagonal map Delta is a well-defined superalgebra homomorphism:
   124	identifying theta1 and theta2 respects all anticommutation/square-zero relations.
   125	Thus augmentation commutes with every operator in the displayed definition,
   126	with multiplication, and with Delta. On f(1)g(2) there is no theta dependence, so the
   127	odd derivatives give zero and the remaining operator is
   128	
   129	    Delta[(theta1 partial_y1-theta2 partial_y2)P f(1)g(2)]
   130	       =theta Delta[(partial_y1-partial_y2)P f(1)g(2)]
   131	       =theta D_y P f·g.
   132	
   133	Since f,g are scalar, `theta h=0` in the free scalar module with basis (1,theta)
   134	forces `h=0`. This proves the implication. No theta derivative is commuted through
   135	diagonal evaluation; the only commutations used are with parameter augmentation. ∎
   136	
   137	**Why nilpotent corrections cannot help.** Every such correction belongs to I.
   138	Applying the even bilinear differential operator or a theta derivative still leaves
   139	at least one parameter generator. It cannot cancel the zeta-degree-zero coefficient
   140	of 1 or theta. Inverting an even coefficient with nonzero body does not change this
   141	argument: augmentation remains an algebra homomorphism on that localization.
   142	No division by an odd/nilpotent element is allowed.
   143	
   144	**Invalid shortcut.** Setting theta=0 together with the parameters would turn the
   145	second expression in (2.1) into zero and lose the constraint. For example, with
   146	`f=x^n y,g=1`, `D_y P_n f·g=n!`, while
   147	`pi_zeta(S_y P_n f·g)=theta n! != 0`. Full theta-zero evaluation would misleadingly
   148	report zero. The replay explicitly checks this negative control.
